<header><p class="st-kicker">crusader state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Galilee</h1><p class="st-meta">1099 CE – 1187 CE · capital Tiberias</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest lordship of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, centred on Tiberias, whose siege in 1187 drew the Frankish army to defeat at Hattin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred</a>, the Norman crusader, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and the surrounding region soon after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1099 and styled himself Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>. When he left for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Antioch</a> in 1101, Baldwin I gave the fief to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-saint-omer">Hugh of Saint-Omer</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> owed the king a large contingent of knights and had its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-toron">Lordship of Toron</a>, whose castle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-saint-omer">Hugh of Saint-Omer</a> built. Its lords collected revenue from fertile farmland around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a> and faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> across the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Jordan</a>. In the 12th century it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bure">Bures</a> family, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschiva-of-bures">Eschiva of Bures</a> to her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In July 1187 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschiva-of-bures">Eschiva</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>. The royal army marched to relieve her and was destroyed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> fell to Saladin. The title survived among exiled Frankish nobles, but the principality was never restored.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-galilee.jpg" alt="Principality of Galilee" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ec.Domnowall · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vexillum_Regni_Hierosolymae.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1099 CE – 1187 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>crusader state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tiberias</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tancred, Hugh of Saint-Omer, Raymond III of Tripoli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Tiberias</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1143&amp;focus=principality-of-galilee" aria-label="Principality of Galilee on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5591 986 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Galilee lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5911 1242L5908 1277M5911 1242L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5911 1242h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5911 1242h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tiberias. <a href="/atlas?year=1143&amp;focus=principality-of-galilee">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

